# Best Table and Depth for Radiant Diamond: The Complete Chart

The GIA does not issue cut grades for radiant cut diamonds. That means when you buy a radiant cut, there is no "Excellent" stamp to rely on — you read the proportions yourself, or you trust someone who knows what they mean.

Most buyers do not read them. Retailers rarely explain them. The result is that a significant portion of radiant cut diamonds sold carry proportions that reduce brilliance or make the stone look smaller than its carat weight suggests.

This guide covers the specific numbers: ideal table percentage, ideal depth percentage, length-to-width ratio, and the full face-up size chart by carat from 0.5ct to 5ct. All proportion data applies to both lab and natural radiant cut diamonds — the physics of light return does not change based on origin.

These numbers matter at every price point. A poorly proportioned stone at $867 is still a poorly proportioned stone.

A well-proportioned stone at $2,318 looks worth every dollar. Read the proportions before the color and clarity — proportions are the foundation everything else rests on.

## TL;DR

- Ideal table percentage for a radiant cut: **61–69%**. Below 57% reduces brilliance; above 73% flattens the crown and kills fire.
- Ideal depth percentage: **61–67%**. Above 70% hides carat weight — the stone looks smaller on the finger than its certificate says.
- L:W ratio defines the shape: 1.00–1.05 is square, 1.25–1.35 is the most popular elongated range, 1.40+ is very elongated.
- Face-up size at 1ct averages approximately 6.5×4.9mm for a 1.25:1 ratio — this is 20–30% smaller than a 1ct round brilliant at the same weight.
- For the full radiant cut shape guide and engagement ring context, see the [radiant cut engagement rings pillar](/radiant-cut-engagement-rings).

## What Makes Radiant Cut Proportions Different?

A radiant cut is a hybrid — it combines the brilliant facet arrangement of a round diamond with a rectangular or square outline. The 70-facet pattern (8 kite, 8 bezel, 8 half, 16 upper girdle, 8 pavilion main, 8 lower girdle, 4 star, 1 table, 1 culet) creates the "crushed-ice" appearance that distinguishes radiant from emerald cut.

That hybrid structure also makes the radiant cut more forgiving of proportion variations than a step cut. An emerald cut with a slightly off-depth will show dark patches in its clear hall-of-mirrors facets. A radiant cut with the same depth deviation scatters the effect across dozens of small facets — it is less obvious, but still measurable in light return.

The result is a shape with a wider acceptable proportion range than most other fancy cuts — but still with a clear optimal zone. Stones outside that zone lose brilliance, appear smaller than their carat weight suggests, or develop dark centers.

The specific numbers for the optimal zone are what most retailers do not publish and most buyers never ask for. That gap is where this guide lives.

![Radiant cut diamond anatomy diagram — 70-facet hybrid pattern, face-up view and side profile](/images/radiant-cut-diamond-anatomy.avif)

The three variables that define a radiant cut's performance are table percentage, depth percentage, and L:W ratio. Together they determine how much of the stone's face-up area is visible above the finger, how much light the stone returns, and what visual shape the stone creates on the hand. A change in any one of the three changes the stone you see.

> **Farzana says:**
>
> Most buyers focus on color and clarity and never look at the proportion numbers. That is backwards for a radiant cut. Color and clarity are visible once you are looking at the stone — proportion affects whether the stone is worth looking at in the first place. A radiant with a 73% depth is dull. A radiant with a 58% table is lifeless. You check the numbers before you look at the stone.

![Radiant cut diamond ideal proportions guide](/images/radiant-cut-diamond-ideal-proportions-featured.avif)

## The Proportion Window

**The Proportion Window** is the table percentage and depth percentage range where radiant cut diamonds return the most light and show the characteristic crushed-ice brilliance the shape is known for.

The table facet is the flat top surface of the diamond — expressed as a percentage of the stone's total width. A larger table means more direct light entry from above but less crown dispersion (fire). A smaller table sends more light through the angled crown facets, which creates more fire but can reduce overall brightness.

The depth is the total height of the stone from table to culet, expressed as a percentage of the stone's width. A deeper stone is heavier for its face-up size — the weight is hiding in the base of the stone rather than showing on top.

![Radiant cut diamond table and depth percentage guide infographic](/images/radiant-cut-diamond-table-depth-guide.avif)

## Radiant Cut Table Percentage Guide

The table percentage for a radiant cut diamond is measured from the GIA or IGI certificate. The number appears under "Measurements" and "Proportions" — it is listed as "Table: XX%."

| Table % | Performance | What this means |
|---------|-------------|-----------------|
| 55% or less | Avoid | Table too small. The crown angles dominate, which creates a dark center. Brilliance is reduced significantly. |
| 56–60% | Acceptable | Below ideal. Stone shows more fire than average but reduced brightness. Acceptable if depth is in the ideal range and the stone is visually confirmed bright. |
| 61–65% | Ideal — balanced | The sweet spot. Optimal balance of brightness (brilliance) and dispersion (fire). Most well-cut radiants land here. |
| 66–69% | Ideal — brightness-forward | Slightly larger table, more direct light entry, higher brightness, slightly less fire. Still within the optimal zone. |
| 70–73% | Acceptable | Just above ideal. Stone will be bright but sacrifice some fire. Still acceptable if the visual performance confirms it in a face-up video. |
| 74% or above | Avoid | Table dominates the crown. Fire is minimal. The stone will look flat and watery under certain lighting. |

**Verdict:** Target table percentage between 61–69% for a radiant cut diamond. Within that range, 61–65% gives the most balanced performance. A stone in the 66–69% range will be slightly brighter and slightly less fiery — both are acceptable. Do not buy below 57% or above 73% without a confirmed face-up video showing strong light return.

## Radiant Cut Depth Percentage Guide

Depth percentage is the single most important proportion number for a radiant cut diamond. A stone with depth above 70% looks meaningfully smaller on the finger than its carat weight suggests — the weight is in the pavilion, which is below the finger and invisible.

| Depth % | Performance | What this means |
|---------|-------------|-----------------|
| 55% or less | Avoid | Too shallow. Light escapes through the pavilion bottom (fish-eye effect). Stone looks glassy and flat. |
| 56–60% | Acceptable — with caution | Slightly shallow. May show some fish-eye in certain lights. Confirm with a face-up video in natural light. |
| 61–64% | Ideal — face-up optimized | The optimal range for maximum face-up size at a given carat weight. Stone sits wide on the finger. |
| 65–67% | Ideal — light-return optimized | Slightly deeper than 61–64% but still within ideal. Light return is strong. Slight face-up size sacrifice vs. 61% depth. |
| 68–70% | Acceptable — borderline | Approaching the weight-hiding zone. A 1ct stone at 69% depth can look like a 0.85ct on the finger. |
| 71–73% | Avoid for face-up buyers | The stone is hiding weight in its pavilion. Looks visibly smaller than its certificate says. |
| 74% or above | Avoid | Significant weight buried below the girdle. Face-up appearance will disappoint at any carat weight. |

**Verdict:** Target depth between 61–67% for a radiant cut diamond. Stones at 61–64% depth maximize face-up size — if you want the stone to look as large as possible on the finger, stay in this range. Stones at 65–67% have slightly better light return in some lighting conditions. Anything above 69% hides meaningful carat weight below the girdle — avoid unless confirmed visually outstanding.

> **Farzana says:**
>
> The depth number is where I see the most buyers get hurt. A [1ct radiant at $2,318](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25995245?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) with a 71% depth looks smaller than a 0.85ct at 63% depth. The certificate says 1ct. The finger says otherwise. Always check depth before anything else on a radiant cut certificate.

## The Ratio Effect

**The Ratio Effect** is the visual transformation that happens as a radiant cut's length-to-width (L:W) ratio changes — from a square outline at 1.00:1 to a strongly elongated shape at 1.50:1 and above.

The L:W ratio is calculated by dividing the longer measurement by the shorter measurement from the certificate. A stone listed as 7.50×5.70mm has an L:W ratio of 7.50 ÷ 5.70 = 1.316 — a classic elongated radiant.

The ratio changes three things simultaneously: the visual shape of the stone, the perceived length of the finger when worn as a ring, and the face-up area at a given carat weight. A more elongated stone (higher L:W) has a larger linear footprint on the finger, which creates an elongating effect — but the same carat weight spreads thinner, which can reduce the "punch" of sparkle from a specific angle.

![Radiant cut diamond length to width ratio chart](/images/radiant-cut-length-width-ratio-chart.avif)

## Radiant Cut Length to Width Ratio Guide

| L:W Ratio | Shape Description | Who It Suits | What this means |
|-----------|------------------|--------------|-----------------|
| 1.00–1.05 | Square radiant | Buyers who prefer a non-round square stone without a princess cut's pointed corners | The square radiant is less common but striking. Square shape with all the crushed-ice brilliance. Fingers under size 5.5 can look overwhelmed. |
| 1.05–1.15 | Nearly square — soft rectangle | Buyers between square and rectangular preference | Not quite square, not quite elongated. The most neutral shape in the radiant range. Works on most hand sizes. |
| 1.15–1.25 | Classic balanced rectangle | Buyers who want a rectangle without strong elongation | The most versatile range for radiant cuts. Clearly rectangular without looking stretched. Works in solitaire, halo, and three-stone settings. |
| 1.25–1.35 | Classic elongated radiant | The most popular choice — most finger-elongating in this range | This is where most buyers land. Visually flattering on a wide range of hand sizes. The 1.30 ratio is often cited as the "sweet spot" for radiant cut rings. |
| 1.35–1.50 | Very elongated radiant | Long fingers, buyers who specifically want maximum elongation | Noticeable elongated shape. Strong finger-lengthening effect. Can look out of proportion on short fingers or wide bands. |
| 1.50+ | Extreme elongation | Buyers who specifically want a near-oval rectangular shape | Uncommon. The stone approaches a cushion-modified rectangular shape at high ratios. Usually a custom preference. |

**Verdict:** The 1.25–1.35 L:W ratio range is the strongest choice for most buyers. It delivers clear elongation without crossing into proportions that look stretched or require a specific hand size to wear well. If you want a square radiant, stay between 1.00–1.05. If maximum elongation is the goal, 1.40–1.50 is the correct target.

## Radiant Cut Diamond Face-Up Size Chart by Carat

The face-up size of a radiant cut diamond depends on three variables: carat weight, depth percentage, and L:W ratio. The chart below assumes a 1.25:1 L:W ratio and 63% depth — the most common configuration for a well-cut radiant cut in certified market inventory.

![Radiant cut diamond face-up size chart by carat weight](/images/radiant-cut-diamond-face-up-size-chart.avif)

| Carat | Length × Width (approx) | Face-Up Area | On the Finger |
|-------|------------------------|--------------|---------------|
| 0.25ct | ~3.8×3.0mm | ~9 mm² | Very small — appropriate for stacking rings |
| 0.50ct | ~5.0×3.8mm | ~15 mm² | Subtle, delicate — promise ring or stacking size |
| 0.75ct | ~5.8×4.4mm | ~20 mm² | Visible, wearable for daily use |
| 1.00ct | ~6.5×4.9mm | ~25 mm² | Clearly present on the finger — the most common promise/entry ring size |
| 1.25ct | ~7.0×5.3mm | ~29 mm² | Noticeably larger than 1ct — visible at conversational distance |
| 1.50ct | ~7.5×5.7mm | ~33 mm² | Clear statement size — [The Solitaire Sweet Spot](/radiant-cut-diamond-necklace) for pendants |
| 1.75ct | ~7.9×6.0mm | ~37 mm² | Approaching statement ring territory |
| 2.00ct | ~8.5×6.4mm | ~42 mm² | Commanding presence — visible across a room |
| 2.50ct | ~9.2×7.0mm | ~50 mm² | Large stone — formal wear or dedicated statement ring |
| 3.00ct | ~10.0×7.5mm | ~58 mm² | Major ring — significant presence in any setting |
| 4.00ct | ~11.2×8.5mm | ~74 mm² | Luxury tier — [requires specific setting support](/radiant-cut-diamond-settings) |
| 5.00ct | ~12.3×9.3mm | ~89 mm² | Collector/investment tier — see the [5ct radiant guide](/5-carat-radiant-cut-diamond-ring) |

**How depth % changes face-up size:** A 1ct radiant at 63% depth measures approximately 6.5×4.9mm. The same 1ct stone at 69% depth measures approximately 6.2×4.6mm — 5% narrower and 6% shorter for the same carat weight. The 6-point depth difference costs you approximately 12% of face-up area.

**How L:W ratio changes dimensions:** At 1ct, a 1.00:1 square radiant measures approximately 5.5×5.5mm. A 1.25:1 elongated measures 6.5×4.9mm. A 1.40:1 very elongated measures approximately 7.0×4.7mm — longer but narrower, with a similar total face-up area.

## Radiant Cut Diamond Sizes: What You Actually See

Radiant cut diamonds appear smaller face-up than round brilliant diamonds of the same carat weight. This is not a flaw — it is a geometry difference.

A round brilliant at 1ct measures approximately 6.4mm in diameter (circular face-up). A radiant cut at 1ct with a 1.25:1 ratio measures approximately 6.5×4.9mm — same length as the round, but 24% narrower in width.

The face-up area comparison tells the true story. A 1ct round brilliant has a face-up area of approximately 32 mm². A 1ct radiant at 1.25:1 ratio and 63% depth has approximately 25 mm² — about 22% less, meaning less diamond visible per carat than a round brilliant of equivalent weight.

This does not mean radiant cuts look small. The rectangular footprint creates a different presence — the stone reads as elongated rather than circular, which has its own visual impact and finger-lengthening effect. But the comparison matters when buyers expect a radiant to look as large as their friend's round diamond of the same carat weight.

| 1ct Comparison | Round Brilliant | Radiant (1.25:1) | Radiant (1.35:1) |
|---------------|-----------------|-----------------|-----------------|
| Diameter/Dimensions | ~6.4mm | ~6.5×4.9mm | ~7.0×4.8mm |
| Face-up area | ~32 mm² | ~25 mm² | ~26 mm² |
| Face-up vs. round | Baseline | ~22% smaller | ~19% smaller |
| Finger footprint | Circular | Elongated rectangle | More elongated |
| What this means | Maximum face-up per carat of any cut | Narrower but elongating — different visual, not smaller in impact | Higher L:W gives longer look; width sacrificed for length |

## Radiant Cut Proportions vs. Other Diamond Cuts

Every fancy shape has its own accepted proportion range — and none of them match the radiant cut's numbers. If you are comparing cuts before deciding, this table shows you where the radiant sits relative to round, emerald, cushion, princess, and oval.

| Cut | Ideal Table % | Ideal Depth % | GIA Cut Grade? | Verdict |
|-----|--------------|---------------|----------------|---------|
| Round Brilliant | 54–58% | 59–63% | Yes — Excellent/Very Good | Only cut with a standardized cut grade. Narrowest acceptable table range of any cut. Use "Excellent" as a shortcut — it covers all proportions at once. |
| Radiant Cut | 61–69% | 61–67% | No | Widest table range of all cuts. No overall grade — must read individual proportion numbers. **[The Proportion Window](#the-proportion-window)** applies here. |
| Emerald Cut | 60–70% | 61–67% | No | Similar depth range to radiant. Wider table because step-cut facets need a larger flat surface to produce the hall-of-mirrors effect. No cut grade — same rule as radiant. |
| Cushion Cut | 56–65% | 61–68% | No | Lower table than radiant; depth overlaps. Brilliant pavilion facets differ from radiant's, so a 67% depth on a cushion looks different to the eye than 67% on a radiant. |
| Princess Cut | 62–69% | 64–75% | No | Accepts much higher depth than radiant — the pyramid pavilion is steeper by design. A 72% depth is acceptable for princess; the same number on a radiant cuts face-up area significantly. |
| Oval Cut | 53–63% | 58–62% | No | Lower table and shallower depth than radiant. Shallower pavilion maximizes the elongated face-up area — the opposite design logic to radiant's deeper pavilion. |

**What this means:** The radiant cut's table range (61–69%) is one of the widest of any cut — which means a retailer can sell you a stone at either end of that range, and both carry the same grading language. The depth range (61–67%) is comparable to emerald and cushion. The meaningful difference: round brilliants give you a shortcut (GIA Excellent); all fancy shapes, including radiant, require you to read the numbers yourself.

**Verdict:** If you are comparing a radiant to an emerald cut, the depth ranges are nearly identical — the visual difference comes from facet pattern (crushed-ice vs. step-cut), not proportions. If you are comparing a radiant to a princess, the depth numbers look similar on paper but behave differently — princess accepts steeper depths without the same face-up penalty. If you are comparing to oval, expect the oval to show more face-up size per carat at the same carat weight, because its shallower depth (58–62%) leaves more diamond visible on top.

## How to Read a Radiant Cut Certificate

A GIA or IGI certificate for a radiant cut diamond lists the proportion data under the "Proportions" section. Here is what to look for and where to find it.

**Measurements:** Listed as Length × Width × Depth in mm. The first number is longer, the second is shorter — divide them for your L:W ratio.

**Table %:** Listed as "Table: XX%." Target 61–69% for [The Proportion Window](#the-proportion-window).

**Depth %:** Listed as "Depth: XX%." Target 61–67%. This is the number that most affects face-up size.

**Girdle:** Listed as a thickness range (e.g., "Thin to Medium" or "Medium to Slightly Thick"). Thin to slightly thick is acceptable. Very thin or extremely thick affects both durability and depth percentage.

**Culet:** Should be "None" or "Very Small." A medium or large culet creates a visible dark circle at the center of the stone when viewed face-up.

**Polish and Symmetry:** GIA and IGI both grade these as Excellent/Very Good/Good. Target Very Good minimum. Avoid Good or below — it indicates inconsistency in the cutting that goes beyond proportion numbers.

**What GIA does NOT grade on fancy shapes:** GIA does not assign an overall cut grade to radiant cut diamonds. Unlike round brilliants (where "Excellent" is a meaningful shortcut), radiant cut quality must be assessed from the individual proportion numbers listed above. IGI also does not grade radiant cut cut quality with an overall grade.

**Why this matters in practice:** When you see a radiant cut listed as "Excellent Polish / Excellent Symmetry" on a certificate, that does not mean the cut is excellent overall. Polish and symmetry are micro-level assessments — they describe the surface finish and facet alignment, not whether the depth or table is in the optimal zone. A stone can have Excellent polish and Excellent symmetry at 73% depth, which is still a poorly proportioned stone. Read both. Neither replaces the other.

**The face-up video rule:** Proportion numbers tell you what to expect; a face-up video confirms whether that specific stone delivers. Radiant cut diamonds vary in how their crushed-ice pattern looks even within the same proportion range — some show a bright, even distribution of sparkle, others show a slightly darker center or uneven fire distribution. For any stone above $1,500, request a face-up video in natural daylight (not studio lighting) before confirming. If the retailer cannot provide one, buy from one who can.

## Who Should Use This Proportion Data

**The online buyer:** You are buying a radiant cut diamond through an online retailer — Blue Nile, Brilliant Earth, James Allen — without seeing the stone in person. The proportion data on the certificate is your only pre-purchase tool. An [E-VVS1 IGI lab radiant at $867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) with a 73% depth is a worse stone than the same grade at 63% depth — and costs the same. Certificate proportions are how you tell them apart.

**The upgrade buyer:** You already own a radiant cut and are wondering whether to keep it or trade it for a better-proportioned stone. Read the proportions off your existing certificate and compare against [The Proportion Window](#the-proportion-window). If your stone is at 70%+ depth, that is the visual reason it may look smaller than you expected for its carat weight.

**The shape-decision buyer:** You are choosing between elongated radiant (1.30–1.40 L:W), square radiant (1.00–1.05), or somewhere between. The [Ratio Effect](#the-ratio-effect) section above shows exactly how each ratio range changes the finger appearance and who each suits.

**The comparison buyer:** You are comparing a radiant cut to an emerald cut, cushion cut, or oval and want to understand why two 1ct stones of the same carat weight look different sizes. The face-up size chart answers this — the cut shape's geometry, depth %, and L:W ratio all affect what you actually see on the finger.

## A Proportion Checklist for Radiant Cut Buyers

Before confirming any radiant cut diamond purchase, verify these five numbers from the certificate.

- → **Table %:** Confirm between 61–69%. Print the certificate and circle this number first.
- → **Depth %:** Confirm between 61–67%. If above 68%, request a face-up video — the stone may appear smaller than its weight suggests.
- → **L:W ratio:** Calculate from the measurements (length ÷ width). Confirm it matches your intended shape preference — square (1.00–1.05), balanced (1.15–1.25), elongated (1.25–1.35), or very elongated (1.35+).
- → **Girdle thickness:** Confirm "Thin to Medium" or "Medium to Slightly Thick." Avoid Very Thin (chips easily) or Extremely Thick (hides weight in girdle).
- → **Culet:** Confirm "None" or "Very Small." A medium or larger culet creates a visible dark spot face-up.
- → **Polish and Symmetry:** Confirm Very Good or Excellent for both. Good or below indicates cutting inconsistency beyond what proportion numbers capture.
- → **Face-up video:** For any stone above $1,500, request a face-up video in natural light. Certificate numbers tell you what to expect — the video confirms whether the specific stone delivers on those numbers.

## Decision Snapshot

| Proportion | Ideal Range | Acceptable Range | Avoid |
|------------|-------------|-----------------|-------|
| Table % | 61–69% | 57–73% | <55% or >74% |
| Depth % | 61–67% | 58–70% | <56% or >71% |
| L:W (square) | 1.00–1.05 | 1.00–1.08 | >1.10 for a square look |
| L:W (elongated) | 1.25–1.35 | 1.20–1.45 | <1.15 or >1.55 for most buyers |
| Girdle | Thin–Medium | Very Thin–Slightly Thick | Extremely Thick or Extremely Thin |
| Culet | None–Very Small | Very Small–Small | Medium or above |
| Polish + Symmetry | Excellent | Very Good | Good or below |
| What this means | The Proportion Window — this is where the best light return, best face-up size, and most balanced appearance are found. Buy here and you will not be disappointed by proportions. | Outside ideal but still acceptable if visually confirmed. Request a face-up video before buying anything outside the ideal range. | These deviations affect appearance in ways that no grading upgrade can fix. A D-IF at 75% depth is still a poorly proportioned stone. |

> **Farzana's Final Word:**
>
> Radiant cut proportions are not complicated — they have two numbers that matter more than everything else. Depth below 68% and table between 61–69%. If both are in range, the stone will perform. If depth is above 70%, walk away from that specific stone regardless of what color or clarity it carries. You will see the depth problem every time you look at the ring, and you cannot fix it after purchase.
>
> The L:W ratio question is different — that is about taste, not performance. There is no wrong ratio, only the ratio that matches your preference. But the depth and table numbers are objective. They either deliver light or they do not.

## Expert Summary

The ideal proportions for a radiant cut diamond are table 61–69% and depth 61–67% — this is [The Proportion Window](#the-proportion-window), the range where the 70-facet hybrid brilliant pattern performs best. Outside this window, stones lose brightness at low table percentages, lose fire at high table percentages, or hide carat weight below the girdle at high depth percentages.

The L:W ratio — [The Ratio Effect](#the-ratio-effect) — determines the stone's visual personality: square at 1.00–1.05, most popular elongated at 1.25–1.35, very elongated at 1.40+. There is no wrong ratio, but ratio affects hand appearance and setting compatibility in ways that proportion numbers alone do not.

The GIA does not grade radiant cut cut quality overall. Every buyer must read the proportion numbers themselves. The face-up size chart by carat (see above) shows what to expect from a well-proportioned radiant at each carat weight — and explains why a radiant cut at the same carat weight as a round brilliant will appear smaller in face-up area.

For the full radiant cut buying guide covering setting options, price data, and shape comparisons, see the [radiant cut engagement rings pillar](/radiant-cut-engagement-rings). All proportion data in this guide is based on industry-standard cutting analysis for radiant cut diamonds as of {{month_year}}.

