## Frequently Asked Questions

### What is the ideal table percentage for a radiant cut diamond?

The ideal table percentage for a radiant cut diamond is 61–69%. Within that range, 61–65% gives the most balanced performance between brilliance and fire.

A table below 57% reduces overall brightness; a table above 73% flattens the crown and eliminates most fire. Unlike round brilliants, the GIA does not issue overall cut grades for radiant cuts — you must check this number yourself on the certificate.

### What is the ideal depth percentage for a radiant cut diamond?

The ideal depth percentage for a radiant cut diamond is 61–67%. Stones below 58% can show a fish-eye effect (light escaping through the pavilion). Stones above 70% hide carat weight below the girdle, making the stone look smaller on the finger than its certificate says — depth is the single most important proportion number to check before buying.

### What is the best length to width ratio for a radiant cut diamond?

The most popular L:W ratio for radiant cut diamonds is 1.25–1.35 — clearly rectangular, noticeably elongating on the finger, and well-proportioned in most ring settings. For a square radiant, target 1.00–1.05. For maximum elongation, 1.35–1.50 creates a significantly lengthened shape — there is no single best ratio, only the ratio that matches your shape preference and hand size.

### How big is a 1 carat radiant cut diamond in mm?

A 1ct radiant cut diamond with a 1.25:1 L:W ratio and approximately 63% depth measures approximately 6.5×4.9mm. At a 1.35:1 ratio, the same carat weight measures approximately 7.0×4.8mm — longer but narrower. A 1ct radiant always measures smaller in face-up area than a 1ct round brilliant (~32 mm² / 6.4mm diameter) — the radiant's face-up at 1ct is approximately 25 mm², about 22% less.

### Does radiant cut look smaller than round?

Yes — a radiant cut at the same carat weight as a round brilliant has approximately 20–25% less face-up area. A 1ct round brilliant is approximately 32 mm² face-up; a 1ct radiant at 1.25:1 ratio is approximately 25 mm². The rectangular shape creates an elongating rather than circular presence — the trade-off for less face-up area is the distinctive crushed-ice sparkle and finger-lengthening effect.

### How do I calculate the L:W ratio on a radiant cut certificate?

Divide the first measurement (longer) by the second measurement (shorter) from the "Measurements" field on the GIA or IGI certificate. Example: a stone listed as "7.50×5.70mm" has an L:W ratio of 7.50 ÷ 5.70 = 1.316 — round to two decimal places, which puts it in the 1.30–1.35 elongated range, a classic elongated radiant.

### What happens if radiant cut depth is too high?

A radiant cut diamond with depth above 70% hides significant carat weight in the pavilion — the base of the stone below the setting's prongs. The weight is there, but it is not visible face-up.

The stone looks smaller on the finger than its carat weight certificate suggests. A 1ct radiant at 73% depth can appear visually similar to a 0.80ct stone at 63% depth. High depth also affects light return — stones above 73% can develop dark areas in the pavilion visible face-up.

### What is the best radiant cut size for an engagement ring?

For an engagement ring, 1ct to 1.5ct is the most common radiant cut range — visible on the finger at conversational distance without crossing into statement territory.

At 1ct (6.5×4.9mm at 1.25:1 ratio), the radiant is clearly present but not commanding. At 1.5ct (7.5×5.7mm), it becomes the centerpiece of any look. At 2ct (8.5×6.4mm), it is a dedicated statement ring — see the [radiant cut engagement rings guide](/radiant-cut-engagement-rings) for full setting and carat recommendations.

### Do lab radiant cut diamonds have different proportions than natural?

No — lab-grown radiant cut diamonds are cut to the same proportion standards as natural diamonds, and the same table, depth, and L:W ratio targets apply. A [1ct lab E-VVS1 IGI radiant at $867](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29417414?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) should have table 61–69% and depth 61–67% just like a natural stone. Check the certificate proportions for a lab stone with the same rigor you would apply to a natural stone.

### What is the difference between radiant cut and princess cut proportions?

Princess cut diamonds (square brilliant) have different ideal proportions: table 62–69%, depth 64–75%. The princess cut's depth range runs higher because its pyramid-shaped pavilion is steeper than a radiant cut's. A princess at 70% depth is within its ideal range; a radiant at 70% is hiding weight — and the radiant's trimmed corners require slightly different girdle thickness considerations than the princess's sharp points.

### Why does GIA not grade radiant cut cut quality?

The GIA only issues overall cut grades for round brilliant diamonds, where the circular symmetry allows a standardized measurement model. Fancy cuts like radiant, oval, pear, and cushion have variable shapes and L:W ratios that resist a single standardized grade. The GIA grades polish and symmetry on fancy shapes but not overall cut — which means every radiant cut buyer must interpret the proportion numbers themselves.

### What radiant cut proportions should I avoid?

Avoid depth above 71% (hides weight, reduces apparent size), table below 57% or above 73% (kills brilliance or fire), Very Thin girdle (chipping risk), and any culet above Small (creates a visible dark center face-up). Also avoid Polish or Symmetry grades of Good or below — these indicate cutting inconsistencies that go beyond what proportion numbers measure. On a fancy cut with no GIA cut grade, these individual parameters are all you have.

